\description{
The dot-named functions from BUCSS 1.x are \strong{deprecated} as
  of BUCSS 2.0.0 and are no longer actively developed. They are retained so
  that scripts written for BUCSS 1.x continue to run. Each one
  translates its arguments to the 2.x convention and calls the corresponding
  \code{ss_buc_*} function, returning that function's
  \code{\link{bucss_plan}} object.

  For backward compatibility the returned object still supports the 1.x
  positional extraction: \code{result[[1]]} is the necessary sample size and
  \code{result[[2]]} is the bias and uncertainty adjusted noncentrality
  parameter, the same two values the 1.x functions returned as an unnamed
  list (this is provided by a \code{[[} method for \code{bucss_plan}). Only
  those two subscripts are kept: code that treats the 1.x result as a list
  in other ways (\code{unlist(result)}, \code{result[[1L]]}, or printing it
  through \code{rbind()}) now sees the data frame. The richer named view
  (\code{result$value}, \code{tidy(result)}, \code{glance(result)}) is also
  available.

  Calling one of these functions issues a deprecation warning that names its
  replacement, once per function per session; the \code{ss_buc_*} functions
  themselves never issue this deprecation warning. New code should call the
  \code{ss_buc_*} functions directly.
}
\details{
The replacements are:

\tabular{ll}{
  \strong{Deprecated} \tab \strong{Replacement} \cr
  \code{ss.power.it} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_independent_t}} (alias \code{\link{ss_buc_smd}}) \cr
  \code{ss.power.dt} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_paired_t}} (alias \code{\link{ss_buc_smd_paired}}) \cr
  \code{ss.power.ba} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_one_way_anova}} (when \code{levels.B} is \code{NULL}) or \code{\link{ss_buc_factorial_anova}} \cr
  \code{ss.power.ba.general} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_factorial_anova_general}} \cr
  \code{ss.power.wa} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_rm_anova}} \cr
  \code{ss.power.wa.general} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_rm_anova_general}} \cr
  \code{ss.power.spa} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_mixed_anova}} \cr
  \code{ss.power.spa.general} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_mixed_anova_general}} \cr
  \code{ss.power.reg1} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_reg_coef}} \cr
  \code{ss.power.reg.all} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_R2}} \cr
  \code{ss.power.reg.joint} \tab \code{\link{ss_buc_reg_joint}} \cr
}

  Because BUCSS 2.0.0 finds the noncentrality parameter by direct root
  finding rather than the fixed 0 to 100 grid used in 1.x, the values these
  wrappers return can differ slightly from 1.x and are no longer capped when
  the implied parameter exceeds 100. The \code{step} argument is accepted for
  call compatibility and ignored.

  Some 1.x behaviors deliberately changed, so a 1.x call can now error where
  it used to return a number. \code{ss.power.ba.general} rejects a
  \code{df.denominator} greater than \code{N - cells} (1.x accepted such a
  value and ignored the argument entirely, including in its own documented
  example). As in 1.x, an \code{assurance} or \code{power} of 1 or more is
  read as a percentage, so exactly 1 means 1 percent; after that reading, an
  \code{assurance} or \code{power} of 0, or of 100 (which means 1), is
  rejected rather than returning an artifact of the internal search. A
  \code{power} at or below \code{alpha.planned} after that reading is not
  allowable and stops too, so \code{power = 1} (which means .01) stops
  unless \code{alpha.planned} is below .01; 1.x returned the smallest
  sample size it considers for such a target, with no notice. An
  \code{alpha.prior} of 1 is computed as exactly 1, where 1.x computed
  with .999 in its place: most such plans are unchanged, and the rest are
  slightly smaller.
  \code{ss.power.ba} and \code{ss.power.wa} refuse \code{effect =
  "factor.B"} or \code{"interaction"} when \code{levels.B} is not given (1.x
  refused only the interaction, and silently returned the Factor A plan for
  \code{"factor.B"}). \code{ss.power.ba.general} and
  \code{ss.power.spa.general} reject numerator degrees of freedom no design
  of their kind can have (\code{df.numerator} above \code{cells - 1}; for
  the split plot, above \code{num.groups - 1} for the between-subjects
  effect, or not a multiple of \code{df.num.within} within that bound for
  the interaction). Non-scalar, non-finite, and fractional inputs are
  rejected with a message naming the argument. Most errors raised through
  these wrappers come from the \code{ss_buc_*} planners and name their
  arguments (\code{t_observed} for \code{t.observed}, and so on); an
  unrecognized \code{effect} is rejected by the wrapper itself. The
  regression wrappers keep the 1.x fixed-predictor computation
  (\code{random_predictors = FALSE}); call \code{\link{ss_buc_reg_coef}},
  \code{\link{ss_buc_R2}}, or \code{\link{ss_buc_reg_joint}} directly for
  predictors sampled along with the outcome. See \code{NEWS} for the full
  accounting.
}
